# Manual jog: drive the robot directly for a SHORT, self-expiring interval.
#
# This is the "reposition it without picking it up" path. Carrying the robot
# by hand breaks SLAM completely — slam_toolbox gates on odometry-reported
# motion, so a lift-and-place reports zero movement and the map and pose stay
# frozen at the old spot (the kidnapped-robot problem; mapping mode has no
# relocalization). Driving instead keeps odometry, TF and the map consistent.
#
# A service rather than a topic, for the same reason as SetWaypoint: it keeps
# rm_dashboard a pure leaf with no publisher of any kind, which is what makes
# it structurally incapable of ever becoming a second /cmd_vel source.
#
# THE TTL IS THE DEADMAN AND IT IS NOT OPTIONAL. The caller is a browser on a
# network. It can close its tab, sleep its laptop, lose WiFi, or simply stop
# sending mid-key-press, and none of those produce a "stop" message. So a jog
# does not mean "drive until told otherwise" -- it means "drive for at most
# ttl_s more". The caller must KEEP RE-SENDING to keep moving; silence stops
# the robot. That gives two independent layers: this TTL expiring (the node
# goes silent), and then rm_safety's own command_timeout zeroing /cmd_vel.

# Body-frame velocities. Clamped by the node to the waypoint controller's
# configured limits, so a browser cannot ask for more than the operator
# configured. Unlike a waypoint, REVERSE IS ALLOWED (negative vx): backing out
# of a corner is exactly what repositioning needs, and there is a human
# watching, which is not true of an autonomous waypoint drive.
float64 vx
float64 wz

# How long this command stays valid, seconds. Bounded by the node.
# Send roughly every ttl_s/2 while a key is held.
float64 ttl_s
---
bool accepted
string message
